10:18:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The “sound barrier” only applies to **objects or disturbances moving through a material medium like air** and pushing the air out of the way. A plane flying faster than sound has its body continually compressing air in front of it. Normally, those pressure changes travel away at the speed of sound. But when the plane is faster than those pressure signals, the compressed air piles up into a shock wave. When that shock wave reaches you, you hear a sonic boom. Light and radio waves are different: - They are **electromagnetic waves**, not chunks of matter plowing through air. - They can travel through a vacuum, where sound cannot travel at all. - They do interact with air a little, but ordinary light has far too little momentum/pressure to noticeably shove the air and form a shock wave. So when you turn on a light, photons spread through the room at nearly the speed of light, but they do not act like a supersonic airplane moving through and compressing the air. No significant shock wave forms, so no sonic boom. Very intense light can affect air: for example, powerful pulsed lasers can heat or ionize air so rapidly that they create a popping sound or even a small shock wave. But that is because the laser deposits a huge amount of energy into the air, not simply because light travels faster than sound.
