10:23:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. Being in the same **speed category** does not mean having the same physical effects. “Things faster than sound” includes very different things: - a bullet: solid matter forcing air aside; - a jet: a huge vehicle compressing air; - a whip tip: a small but sharp moving air disturbance; - light/radio: electromagnetic waves, usually barely affecting the air. The boom is not an effect of the label *faster than sound* alone. It is an effect of **supersonic compression of air**. Bullets and jets are the famous examples, so it is easy to associate the whole category with booms—but the association is not universal. Like “things hotter than boiling water”: steam and a red-hot metal can both qualify, yet they do not behave identically. The shared number is real, but it does not determine every consequence. So light belongs in the “faster than sound” category, but ordinary light does not belong in the “makes an audible sonic boom” category.
